<h1>Video 084 — Thread Priorities</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Multi Threading Part-4 || Thread Priorities</p>
<p>Source: real YouTube auto-captions (.transcripts/084-DpM_wER1gAY.txt). ASR: keep subing = keep copying; enter your room / ENT = interview; mean priority = MIN_PRIORITY; scheder = scheduler; Tropic / M signal / Sagar Junction = traffic signal analogy; CM / PM = Chief Minister / Prime Minister; parent thread ≠ parent class; Windows 7 platform note at end.</p>
<h3>00:08 — Heading: Thread priorities</h3>
<p>Every thread in Java has some priority — default priority generated by JVM, or customized priority provided by the programmer (another thread property, like name).</p>
<h3>01:11 — Valid range: 1 to 10 (not 0 to 10)</h3>
<p>Big confusion: 0–10 vs 1–10. Priority is not an array index.</p>
<p>Valid range of thread priorities = 1 to 10 (not 0 to 10).</p>
<h3>02:16 — Which is min / max? (EAMCET rank story)</h3>
<p>Village Panchayat / library newspaper story: two illiterate parents confuse ranks — one kid “worst” rank 2500 (actually good for EAMCET), other “best” 148,000 (actually bad). Moral: priorities are not ranks.</p>
<p>Among 1 to 10:</p>
<ul><li>1 = minimum priority</li></ul>
<ul><li>10 = maximum priority</li></ul>
<p>Do not get confused (higher number = higher priority, unlike competitive-exam ranks).</p>
<h3>07:15 — Thread class constants</h3>
<p>Thread.MIN_PRIORITY   // 1
Thread.NORM_PRIORITY  // 5
Thread.MAX_PRIORITY   // 10</p>
<h3>08:18 — Board copy: every thread has some priority</h3>
<ul><li>Every thread in Java has some priority — default priority generated by JVM or customized priority provided by programmer.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Valid range of thread priorities is 1 to 10, where 1 is min priority and 10 is max priority.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Thread class defines the following constants to represent some standard priorities:</li></ul>
<p>Thread.MIN_PRIORITY    // 1
Thread.NORM_PRIORITY   // 5
Thread.MAX_PRIORITY    // 10</p>
<h3>11:40 — Valid vs invalid constant / value quiz</h3>
<p>He lists candidates like high priority, low priority, MIN, NORM, values 0 and 1 and 10:</p>
<p>There is MIN / NORM / MAX, not low / high.</p>
<h3>13:15 — Why priorities? Traffic-signal / VIP analogy</h3>
<p>Four-way traffic signal (M signal / Sagar Junction style). If all equal → clockwise / anticlockwise time slots.</p>
<p>If a minister comes on one side → police give that side highest priority first.</p>
<p>Escalation he builds:</p>
<ul><li>Minister vs normal people → minister first.</li></ul>
<ul><li>CM vs minister → CM first, then minister, then normal.</li></ul>
<ul><li>PM vs CM vs minister → PM, then CM, then minister, then normal.</li></ul>
<ul><li>President of India vs PM… → President first, then PM, CM, minister…</li></ul>
<p>Whoever has highest priority gets the chance first when many fight for the chance.</p>
<p>Same for processor: multiple threads waiting → thread scheduler uses priorities; thread with highest priority gets the chance first.</p>
<p>Numeric example on board (ASR messy on the middle values): threads with different priorities — scheduler prefers the highest priority first, then next, and so on. Core rule: highest priority gets the chance first.</p>
<p>If two threads have the same priority (e.g. both 7): we can’t expect exact order — depends on thread scheduler (round robin / FCFS / …).</p>
<p>Board:</p>
<ul><li>Thread scheduler will use priorities while allocating processor.</li></ul>
<ul><li>The thread which is having highest priority will get the chance first.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If two threads having same priority then we can’t expect exact execution order — it depends on thread scheduler.</li></ul>
<h3>20:59 — getPriority / setPriority</h3>
<p>Same idea as getName / setName:</p>
<pre class='code'>public final int getPriority()
public final void setPriority(int p)</pre>
<p>Allowed values range: 1 to 10. Otherwise runtime exception: IllegalArgumentException.</p>
<p>t.setPriority(7);  // valid

t.setPriority(17); // IllegalArgumentException</p>
<p>Board methods + note:</p>
<pre class='code'>// Thread class defines the following methods to get and set priority of a thread
public final int getPriority()
public final void setPriority(int p)
// allowed values range 1 to 10
// otherwise runtime exception: IllegalArgumentException</pre>
<h3>26:49 — Default priority (trap question)</h3>
<p>He asks: “Default priority for every thread is 5” or “default priority only for main is 5”?</p>
<p>More specific answer students pick: only for main — and that is correct.</p>
<ul><li>Default priority only for the main thread is 5.</li></ul>
<ul><li>For all remaining threads, default priority is inherited from parent to child — whatever priority parent thread has, child gets the same by default.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If parent priority is 10 → child 10; if parent 7 → child 7.</li></ul>
<h3>31:15 — Demo program: inherit priority; illegal 15; comment line</h3>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getPriority());
        // prints 5

        // Thread.currentThread().setPriority(15);
        // IllegalArgumentException

        Thread.currentThread().setPriority(7); // line one (he varies 7 / 8 / 10)
        // now main priority = 7

        MyThread t = new MyThread(); // executed by main → main is parent THREAD
        System.out.println(t.getPriority());
        // prints 7  (inherited — NOT automatically 5)
    }
}</pre>
<p>Critical distinction:</p>
<ul><li>Parent class of MyThread = Thread (extends Thread).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Parent thread of child t = whichever thread executed new MyThread() → main thread.</li></ul>
<p>If you comment the setPriority line (line one), main stays at 5 → child also prints 5.</p>
<p>He also demos setPriority(15) or 17 → compiles, runtime IllegalArgumentException.</p>
<h3>43:24 — Last example: different priorities and expected output</h3>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;Child Thread&quot;);
        }
    }
}

class ThreadPrioritiesDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.setPriority(10); // line one
        t.start();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;Main Thread&quot;);
        }
    }
}</pre>
<p>If commenting line one: both main and child have priority 5 → can’t expect execution order / exact output (mixed possible).</p>
<p>If not commenting line one: main priority 5, child priority 10 → child should get chance first → expected:</p>
<p>Child Thread  (10 times)
Main Thread   (10 times)</p>
<h3>52:27 — Platform twist (Windows 7)</h3>
<p>He runs the program: with equal priorities, sometimes Main then Child, sometimes reverse — no guarantee.</p>
<p>With setPriority(10) he expects Child ×10 then Main ×10, but on his Windows 7 machine he still sees Main first / mixed (Main Child Main…). He saved the file — not a save mistake.</p>
<p>Reason: some operating systems / platforms won’t provide proper support for thread priorities. Windows 7 (his demo OS) by default may not honor priorities as expected.</p>
<p>Joke: send mail to Microsoft asking for a patch; use licensed versions, etc.</p>
<p>Board note:</p>
<p>Some platforms won’t provide proper support for thread priorities.</p>
<p>If on your system you don’t get the discussed output, don’t worry — problem may be the underlying platform, not the program. Change system / platform and support may appear.</p>
<h3>58:19 — Close of Part-4</h3>
<p>That is the thread priorities concept. Terminology clear for next multi-threading topics.</p>
